---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const repo = 'https://github.com/lexwebb/moto-hud';
---
<BaseLayout title="Home" description="Motorcycle e-ink turn-by-turn HUD — sparse nav you can glance at">
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Open hardware · Pi Zero · Inky pHAT</p>
    <h1>Moto HUD</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Turn-by-turn and media on a sunlight-readable e-ink panel — while Google Maps
      and music stay on the phone where they belong.
    </p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="cta primary" href={`${base}emulator/`}>Try the ride emulator</a>
      <a class="cta" href={repo}>View on GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Why</h2>
    <p>
      Phones are great navigators and terrible motorcycle instruments. Bright LCDs wash out
      in sun, notifications fight for attention, and glancing down at a mount is the opposite
      of head-up riding. Moto HUD is a deliberately sparse secondary display: distance,
      maneuver, road name, and optional media — 1-bit, slow to redraw, easy to read at speed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hard problems (routing, traffic, voice, playlist) stay in Maps and your music app.
      The Pi only paints what you need between glances, and three physical buttons cover
      mode / action without touchscreen theatre.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      An Android companion listens to Google Maps notifications and the media session, then
      sends state over BLE to a Go service on a Raspberry Pi Zero. That service renders a
      250×122 Terminus Bold layout onto a Pimoroni Inky pHAT. Prev / Next / Action on GPIO
      change screens or skip / pause without unlocking the phone.
    </p>
    <pre class="flow" aria-label="System flow">{`Maps + media  →  Android companion  →  BLE  →  Go on Pi Zero  →  Inky pHAT
Buttons (GPIO) ─────────────────────────────────────────────↗`}</pre>
    <ul class="plain-list">
      <li><strong>Phone</strong> — source of truth for nav and audio</li>
      <li><strong>Companion app</strong> — NotificationListener + MediaController → BLE</li>
      <li><strong>Pi Zero</strong> — Go HUD core, SPI display, button bridge</li>
      <li><strong>E-ink</strong> — redraw gated (~50&nbsp;m / maneuver), not a video feed</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What’s next</h2>
    <p>
      The software stack and a bench enclosure are in place. On-bike hardening is the focus:
    </p>
    <ul class="plain-list">
      <li>Two-part dock: bike plate stays on the motorcycle; HUD pod pulls off on magnets with 5&nbsp;V pogo</li>
      <li>Weather sealing, lens, and USB-C from the accessory port into the plate (Pi Zero W)</li>
      <li>Exact switch footprints and a tighter case fit from printed prototypes</li>
      <li>Polish on the BLE protocol and companion UX for real rides</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Explore</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">Static demos on this site — no Pi required.</p>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <a class="card" href={`${base}enclosure/`}>
        <h3>Enclosure</h3>
        <p>Orbit the parametric OpenSCAD bench case for Pi Zero + Inky.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href={`${base}design/`}>
        <h3>Design kit</h3>
        <p>Interactive 1-bit HUD mock screens (browser monospace stand-in).</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href={`${base}emulator/`}>
        <h3>Ride emulator</h3>
        <p>Leaflet map + Go WASM HUD core with e-ink refresh emulation.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="note">
    Live pixel preview (<code>/frame.png</code>) and font specimens still need a local
    <code>motohud</code> process — run <code>./scripts/emu.sh</code> from the
    <a href={repo}>repo</a>. This site ships the static demos and the WASM ride sim.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
